id: k8s.exec
kind: kubernetes
title: exec into a container
aliases: kubectl exec, shell into pod
tags: operations, diagnostics
questions:
  - how do you debug inside a running pod?
invariant: exec starts a process in an existing container namespace, so it sees the container's network and filesystem but changes nothing about the image.
answer_30s: `kubectl exec -it pod -- sh` runs a shell inside the container so you can test connectivity and read the live filesystem.
answer_2m: |
  Two things make it useful: the network namespace (so `curl`, `nslookup`, `nc`
  test the same path the app uses) and the mounted config and secrets. Two
  things make it awkward: distroless/minimal images have no shell at all, and
  anything you install disappears with the container. For those cases use a
  debug container (`kubectl debug`) sidecar, or an ephemeral pod with the same
  labels -- which is also the answer when the problem is that the app cannot be
  reached rather than that it cannot run.
when_to_use:
  - verifying network paths, mounted config, and filesystem state inside the real container
commands:
  - kubectl exec -it api-7d9f-abc -- sh => interactive shell inside the container
  - kubectl exec api-7d9f-abc -- env => what the process actually sees (config, secrets)
  - kubectl debug api-7d9f-abc -it --image=nicolaka/netshoot => debug a distroless container with tooling
failure_modes:
  - treating changes made in a shell as permanent configuration
  - debugging a distroless image with no shell available
related:
  - k8s.logs
  - k8s.port-forward
  - k8s.debugging-workflow
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/generated/kubectl_exec/
